[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Decorator vs. Proxy Pattern: Use Cases and Differences"},{"order":2,"type":"subheading","text":"Introduction"},{"order":3,"type":"paragraph","text":"In object-oriented software design, structural patterns help assemble objects and classes into larger structures while keeping these structures flexible and efficient. Two of the most commonly confused structural design patterns are the Decorator and the Proxy. While they share similar structural blueprints—both involve wrapping a core object and implementing the same interface—their intent and core use cases are fundamentally different."},{"order":4,"type":"subheading","text":"Core Concepts"},{"order":5,"type":"paragraph","text":"To understand the difference, we must first look at their primary intent:"},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Decorator Pattern: Dynamically adds responsibilities to an object without altering its underlying code. It focuses on enhancement."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Proxy Pattern: Controls access to an object, often managing its creation, lifecycle, or permissions. It focuses on control."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"The Decorator Pattern in Action"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Decorator pattern lets you attach new behaviors to objects by placing these objects inside special wrapper objects that contain the behaviors. This is a flexible alternative to subclassing."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"interface Notifier {
    void send(String message);
}

class EmailNotifier implements Notifier {
    public void send(String message) {
        System.out.println("Sending email: " + message);
    }
}

abstract class NotifierDecorator implements Notifier {
    protected Notifier wrapped;
    public NotifierDecorator(Notifier n) { this.wrapped = n; }
    public void send(String message) { wrapped.send(message); }
}

class SMSDecorator extends NotifierDecorator {
    public SMSDecorator(Notifier n) { super(n); }
    public void send(String message) {
        super.send(message);
        System.out.println("Sending SMS: " + message);
    }
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"The Proxy Pattern in Action"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Proxy pattern provides a surrogate or placeholder for another object to control access to it. Proxies are commonly used for lazy initialization, access control, logging, or remote execution."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"interface Database {
    void query(String sql);
}

class RealDatabase implements Database {
    public void query(String sql) {
        System.out.println("Executing query: " + sql);
    }
}

class SecurityProxyDatabase implements Database {
    private RealDatabase realDb;
    private String userRole;

    public SecurityProxyDatabase(String role) {
        this.userRole = role;
    }

    public void query(String sql) {
        if ("ADMIN".equals(userRole)) {
            if (realDb == null) { realDb = new RealDatabase(); }
            realDb.query(sql);
        } else {
            throw new SecurityException("Access Denied");
        }
    }
}"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Differences"},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Intent: Decorator enhances functionality. Proxy controls access."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Composition: A decorator usually takes the wrapped object via the constructor and expects the client to pass it. A proxy often manages the lifecycle of the real object internally (e.g., lazy loading it)."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Chaining: Decorators are almost always chained together (e.g., File -> Gzip -> Encrypted). Proxies are rarely chained in this manner; they usually sit as a single gatekeeper."},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"While structural lookalikes, the Decorator and Proxy patterns serve completely different architectural goals. Use the Decorator when you want to dynamically add behaviors to code without subclassing. Use the Proxy when you need a surrogate to protect, delay, or manage access to a resource."}]
